WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:13.470
<v Ira Glass>Tito's 18, lives in Los Angeles, lean and handsome and dark with a bear claw tattooed onto each cheek. And when he talks about his life thus far, he says he just didn't have a choice about certain things.
<v Ira Glass>Tito今年18岁，住在洛杉矶，身材瘦削，长相英俊，肤色黝黑，双颊各纹着一个熊爪纹身。当谈到他迄今为止的生活时，他说自己对某些事情别无选择。

00:00:13.470 --> 00:01:03.900
<v Tito>Basically, when I was locked up in juvenile detention I was there for a year and I really believe that if you locked up a year just with a whole bunch of guys, it really can mess with your mental stage. Because I act all hard. I act like a dude. Most likely I dressed like a guy. I tried to do everything like a guy. Play basketball. But deep inside, these real males can really know that. It's just an act. It's like really, you're a girl, but you're acting like a dude.
<v Tito>基本上，当我在少年拘留所被关了一年的时候，我真的相信，如果你和一大群男人关在一起一年，这真的会扰乱你的精神状态。因为我表现得很强硬。我表现得像个男人。多数时候我穿得像个男人。我试着像男人一样做所有事。打篮球。但内心深处，这些真正的男人其实能看出来。这只是表演。真的，你是个女孩，但你在装男人。

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:47.040
<v Tito>It can be very, very, very frustrating and confusing when you are around a whole bunch of males. Because what starts to happen when you're around other males, it's like the deep side of you, the female part's trying to come out, and just try to say something. But deep inside, you're pushing that person back down. It's like the egg will crack and the female is becoming birthed. I know it sounds very freaky, but this is totally real.
<v Tito>当你被一大群男性包围时，这会非常、非常、非常令人沮丧和困惑。因为当你和别的男人在一起时，你内心深处，女性的那一面就会试图显露出来，试图说点什么。但内心深处，你又把那个人压了回去。这就像蛋壳会裂开，女性特质正在破茧而出。我知道这听起来很诡异，但这完全是真实的。

00:01:47.040 --> 00:02:13.510
<v Ira Glass>If you're trying to figure out who you are, it's funny what can inspire you. What can be helpful and give you strength. Tito, for example, is an Eminem fan. The night Eminem performed at the Grammy's, drunk protesters, gay protesters, who called him homophobic and misogynist. Tito was at a party with gay kids and bisexual kids and transsexual kids. Kids who are actually born as boys but live as girls, who were all watching the Grammy's on TV and cheering for Eminem. Tito explains it this way.
<v Ira Glass>如果你正试图弄清楚自己是谁，那么能启发你的东西会很有趣。什么东西能提供帮助并给你力量。以Tito为例，他是Eminem的粉丝。在Eminem出席格莱美奖的那天晚上，喝醉的抗议者，同性恋抗议者，称他为恐同者和厌女者。Tito当时正和一些同性恋、双性恋和变性人的孩子在派对上。这些孩子天生是男孩，但以女孩的身份生活，他们都在电视上看格莱美奖，并为Eminem欢呼。Tito是这样解释的。

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:25.050
<v Ira Glass>Here's this white guy, Eminem, who somehow felt that part of black culture was just inside him, and decided that he would rap like a black guy. And ended up able to pass in that world.
<v Ira Glass>你看，这个白人家伙，Eminem，他以某种方式感觉黑人文化的一部分就在他心里，并决定他要像个黑人一样说唱。最后居然在那个世界里通行无阻。

00:02:25.050 --> 00:03:00.720
<v Tito>Transgender people, it's the same way. It's a boy that feels like a woman inside. And want to be free to go ahead and explore that. So they want to dress like a girl and acting like girl. It's in the same category for somebody that feels black inside. I feel so. I'm Caucasian, but I feel black inside, and I want to explore my side. And I think if Eminem can do it, anybody can. I mean, transgender should too.
<v Tito>变性人也是一样。那是一个内心感觉像女人的男孩。并想要自由地继续探索这一点。所以他们想穿得像女孩，表现得像女孩。这和那些内心感觉是黑人的人属于同一范畴。我就有这种感觉。我是白人，但我内心感觉自己是黑人，我想探索我这一面。而且我认为如果Eminem能做到，任何人都能做到。我的意思是，变性人也应该可以。

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:36.260
<v Ira Glass>There's a deep part of American culture, which is all about the idea that you can make yourself into anyone. How many of our movies are about the idea that I'm going to be a star? I'm going to sing, dance, take Wall Street by storm. Own this crazy town. See my name in lights. Someday be a gangster. Someday be a real boy. Someday get to Washington and make a difference. Someday leave my uncle's farm in Tatooine and become a Jedi knight like my father. Well, today on our program, stories of people who felt this very American call to be something that they were not, and who then willed it to happen. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>美国文化中有一部分深深扎根于一种观念，即你可以把自己变成任何人。我们有多少部电影是关于“我要成为明星”这个想法的？我要唱歌、跳舞、席卷华尔街。拥有这座疯狂的城市。让我的名字闪耀在灯光下。有一天成为黑帮老大。有一天成为一个真正的男孩。有一天去华盛顿做出改变。有一天离开叔叔在塔图因的农场，像我父亲一样成为绝地武士。好吧，今天我们的节目，讲述了一些人感到这种非常美国的召唤，想要成为他们本来不是的样子，并最终让它实现。来自芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台，这是《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass。

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:46.500
<v Ira Glass>Our program today in three acts. Act One, Girls, Girls, Girls. The story of teenagers who were born as boys, but live as girls in Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目分为三幕。第一幕，女孩，女孩，女孩。讲述了在洛杉矶，天生是男孩但以女孩身份生活的青少年们的故事。

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:57.620
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Agent to the Stars. A fan's unlikely dream to meet his favorite movie star accidentally works. Partly to his delight, partly to his horror.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，明星经纪人。一个粉丝想要见到他最喜欢的电影明星的不可思议的梦想，竟然意外地实现了。部分让他欣喜，部分让他恐惧。

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:15.050
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Airel Sharon, Shimon Peres, David Ben Gurion, and me! An American teenager who dreams someday of becoming the prime minister of a nation where he does not even reside. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，阿里尔·沙龙，希蒙·佩雷斯，大卫·本·古里安，还有我！一个梦想着有一天成为一个他甚至不住在那里的国家的总理的美国少年。请继续收听。

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:18.800
<v Ira Glass>Act one, Girls, Girls, Girls.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，女孩，女孩，女孩。

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:33.130
<v Ira Glass>Over the last 10 years in Los Angeles, there has been a noticeable increase in the number of transsexual teenagers. These are both boys and girls. You see them on the streets. Both are on the rise, but the boys who live as girls are a little more visible.
<v Ira Glass>过去10年里，在洛杉矶，变性青少年的数量有了显著增长。他们既有男孩也有女孩。你在街上能看到他们。两者都在增加，但那些以女孩身份生活的男孩稍微明显一点。

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:46.250
<v Ira Glass>As you might expect, they prefer to be called "she" not "he." And "girls" not "boys." Cris Beam has spent the last two years getting to know these kids, interviewing them, writing about them, and she tells this story.
<v Ira Glass>正如你所料，他们更喜欢被称为“她”而不是“他”。被称为“女孩”而不是“男孩”。Cris Beam过去两年一直在了解这些孩子，采访他们，书写他们，她讲述了这个故事。

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:58.640
<v Cris Beam>I'm writing a book about transsexual youth. In Los Angeles where I live, there are hundreds of them, noisily laughing on street corners. Runaways or castoffs from families who can't possibly understand.
<v Cris Beam>我正在写一本关于变性青少年的书。在我居住的洛杉矶，有成百上千个这样的孩子，在街角喧闹地笑着。他们是离家出走者，或是被那些无法理解他们的家庭抛弃的孩子。

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:24.280
<v Cris Beam>There have always been transsexuals in Los Angeles and everywhere else, but never so many kids, so visible and so determined. They come here because they hear they can find underground hormones, or friends. Or simply because this is the biggest city they can think of. And because they fall under the radar of regular social services, on the street they're playing doctor, beautician, health advisor and shrink to one another.
<v Cris Beam>洛杉矶和其他任何地方一直都有变性人，但从来没有这么多孩子，这么显眼，这么坚定。他们来到这里是因为听说可以找到地下的荷尔蒙，或者朋友。或者仅仅因为这是他们能想到的最大的城市。而且因为他们处于常规社会服务机构的雷达之外，在街头上，他们互为医生、美容师、健康顾问和心理医生。

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:50.260
<v Cris Beam>I posted fliers around asking them to share their stories. I got one call from a 20 year old woman who agreed to meet with me. Her name was Foxxjazell and she told me she was going to be the first international transsexual pop star. She described herself so I could recognize her. I'm very beautiful, she said. I'm tall and black with butter pecan caramel skin and long, luxurious, silky hair. She suggested we meet at the Red Lobster.
<v Cris Beam>我到处张贴传单，请求他们分享自己的故事。我接到了一个20岁女性的电话，她同意和我见面。她的名字叫Foxxjazell，她告诉我她将成为第一个国际变性流行歌星。她描述了自己的样子以便我能认出她。我非常漂亮，她说。我身材高挑，是黑人，有着黄核桃仁焦糖色的皮肤和又长、又奢华、如丝绸般的头发。她建议我们在红龙虾餐厅见面。

00:05:50.260 --> 00:06:02.690
<v Cris Beam>When I got there, I saw she wasn't lying. She was really pretty. She wore glitter on her eyes and had silver charms braided into her hair. On her ripped jeans she had scrawled the words "Latins do it better."
<v Cris Beam>当我到那里时，我发现她没有撒谎。她真的很漂亮。她的眼睛上涂着闪粉，头发里编着银色的装饰品。她那条破洞牛仔裤上潦草地写着“拉丁裔做得更好”。

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:27.270
<v Cris Beam>She'd grown up Dwight Eric Jackson in Birmingham, Alabama. As early as she can remember, she felt like a girl. When the teacher would parcel kids off into boy and girl teams in kindergarten, Foxx stood there frozen, not knowing which group to join. And when she hit adolescence, Foxx had to invent herself like any other teenager, but without the templates-- prom queen, brainiac, jock.
<v Cris Beam>她在阿拉巴马州伯明翰长大，原名Dwight Eric Jackson。从她记事起，她就觉得自己像个女孩。在幼儿园，当老师把孩子们分成男生组和女生组时，Foxx站在那里，不知所措，不知道该加入哪个组。当她进入青春期时，Foxx不得不像其他任何青少年一样创造自己，但却没有模板——舞会皇后、优等生、运动员。

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:40.560
<v Cris Beam>Foxx had to make up a whole persona that fit with the gender inside her. It's a trait that attracts me to these transgender kids in general. Their intense drive to develop their own reality, and then their own rules to get there.
<v Cris Beam>Foxx必须编造出一个完全符合她内心性别的角色。这种特质吸引我关注这些变性孩子。他们开发自己现实的强烈驱动力，以及为了实现这一点而制定的规则。

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:47.170
<v Cris Beam>For instance, for a few years in high school, inspired by Prince, Foxx replaced her own name with a symbol.
<v Cris Beam>例如，高中几年里，受Prince启发，Foxx用一个符号代替了自己的名字。

00:06:47.170 --> 00:07:23.710
<v Foxxjazell>I did not use my name at all. I would use a pink triangle. I would always carry this pink marker around with me every day. And if I wanted to sign my name or did my homework or something like that, I'll put a pink triangle on there. Because the pink triangle was originally from the Holocaust. Because back then, a lot of the gay people there in the concentration camps got pink triangles put on them. But being the creative soul that I am, I put something a little bit different on there. I put a sign of a woman on there. And don't you know, it's like the same sign that you see like when you go into the women's bathroom it has that little symbol of a woman.
<v Foxxjazell>我根本不用自己的名字。我用一个粉色三角形。我每天总是随身带着这支粉色马克笔。如果我想签名，或者做家庭作业之类的，我就会在上面画一个粉色三角形。因为粉色三角形最初源于大屠杀。因为那时，集中营里很多同性恋者被标记上粉色三角形。但作为一个富有创造力的灵魂，我在上面加了一点不同的东西。我在上面加了一个女性的符号。你难道不知道吗，这就像你进女厕所时看到的那个女性小符号一样。

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:25.060
<v Cris Beam>Like the woman in a skirt?
<v Cris Beam>就像那个穿着裙子的女人？

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:52.480
<v Foxxjazell>Yes, exactly. It looked exactly like that. And that's the symbol I adapted. And I even got a necklace like that and people would ask me, what does that mean? What does that mean? Everyone would ask me that. And I would love when people would ask me that because I would love to just tell them. I would say this. I would say, it means that I'm a woman trapped inside of a male's body. That's what it means.
<v Foxxjazell>是的，完全正确。看起来一模一样。那就是我采纳的符号。我甚至买了一条那样的项链，人们会问我，那是什么意思？那是什么意思？每个人都会问我。我喜欢人们问我，因为我喜欢直接告诉他们。我会这么说。我会说，这意味着我是一个被困在男性身体里的女人。这就是它的意思。

00:07:52.480 --> 00:08:10.260
<v Cris Beam>Foxx cross-dressed in secret. And alone in her room, pretended to be Janet Jackson. Sometimes she'd sneak out her window late at night in cheap Halloween wigs and halter tops, and just walk around her neighborhood. She wouldn't run into anybody, she just liked how it felt.
<v Cris Beam>Foxx私下里进行异装。在房间里独自一人时，她假装自己是Janet Jackson。有时她深夜会戴着廉价的万圣节假发，穿着挂脖上衣从窗户溜出去，在街区散步。她不会遇到任何人，她只是喜欢那种感觉。

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:40.540
<v Cris Beam>Unlike the parents of many of these kids who will throw them out at first discovery of a stiletto in a school bag, Foxx's parents were supportive of their only child. They just worried for her safety. But she worried for her sanity. She knew she had to go someplace where the unusual was more usual. So as soon as she graduated, she got a job, saved her cash, and bought a bus ticket to Los Angeles. And this is where her story really begins.
<v Cris Beam>不像很多在学校包里发现高跟鞋时就把孩子赶出家门的父母，Foxx的父母对他们唯一的孩子很支持。他们只是担心她的安全。但她担心自己的精神状态。她知道她必须去一个不寻常的事物更常见的地方。所以她一毕业，就找了工作，存了钱，买了一张去洛杉矶的巴士票。而这正是她故事真正开始的地方。

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:51.870
<v Foxxjazell>Cahuenga and Hollywood Boulevard is where I  first got off. And this is where I had said to myself, my name was going to be paved down there on one of these stars one of these days.
<v Foxxjazell>卡温加和好莱坞大道是我第一次下车的地方。我对自己说，总有一天，我的名字会被镶在那里的某一颗星上。

00:08:51.870 --> 00:09:00.620
<v Cris Beam>The corner of Cahuenga and Hollywood is part of the famous Hollywood Walk of Stars. Every square of sidewalk is embedded with a polished star and a name.
<v Cris Beam>卡温加和好莱坞路口是著名的好莱坞星光大道的一部分。每一块人行道都镶嵌着一颗抛光的星星和一个名字。

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:11.930
<v Foxxjazell>Madge Bellamy, I don't know who that is. Harry von Zell, I mean, Alfred Green. If these people were so famous, why haven't I ever heard of them? Oh my God.
<v Foxxjazell>Madge Bellamy，我不知道那是谁。Harry von Zell，还有Alfred Green。如果这些人这么出名，为什么我从来没听说过他们？天哪。

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:34.260
<v Cris Beam>Foxx was just 18 when she got to LA, but she arranged to stay in a group home called Covenant House, where a lot of stray gay kids go. In some ways, it's easier for trans kids to hook up with other girls like themselves than it has ever been. Foxx found where to stay in LA from the gay yellow pages before she ever left Birmingham. Some trans kids use the internet to figure this out.
<v Cris Beam>Foxx到洛杉矶时才18岁，但她安排住在一家名为Covenant House的收容所，那里有很多流浪的同性恋孩子。在某些方面，变性孩子比以往任何时候都更容易联系到和自己一样的女孩。Foxx在离开伯明翰之前就通过同性恋黄页找到了在洛杉矶的住处。有些变性孩子利用互联网来搞清楚这些。

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:49.720
<v Cris Beam>But the jump to full tilt womanhood came on a casual Friday at the telemarketing firm where Foxx was working at the time. On Fridays, employees were told they could wear whatever was comfortable. Most people showed up in jeans and loafers. Foxx showed up as a woman.
<v Cris Beam>但向全职女性身份的转变发生在一个周五，当时Foxx在一家电话营销公司工作。在周五，员工被告知可以穿任何舒适的衣服。大多数人穿着牛仔裤和乐福鞋就来了。Foxx则以女性身份出现。

00:09:49.720 --> 00:10:15.890
<v Foxxjazell>It finally just came down. And it was just like Foxxjazell. I mean all those years trapped inside of that boy's body. I mean, 19 years to be exact. She just couldn't take anymore. She just couldn't take just dressing up on nights, just dressing up on weekends. She wanted to be out all the time.
<v Foxxjazell>它最终还是发生了。就像Foxxjazell一样。我的意思是，被困在那个男孩身体里这么多年。准确地说是19年。她实在受不了了。她再也受不了仅仅在晚上打扮，仅仅在周末打扮。她想一直以女性身份示人。

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:24.450
<v Cris Beam>Since that day last January, Foxx never dressed as a man again. She's fully Foxx, even when she goes home to Alabama for Christmas.
<v Cris Beam>从去年一月的那天起，Foxx再也没有穿过男装。她完全是Foxx，即使她圣诞节回到阿拉巴马州老家时也是如此。

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:27.210
<v Cris Beam>Then what happened? Did you decide to go on hormones pretty soon after that?
<v Cris Beam>然后发生了什么？你决定在那之后很快就开始注射荷尔蒙吗？

00:10:27.210 --> 00:11:04.810
<v Foxxjazell>It would be five months to be exact before I actually started hormones. I went to the LA free clinic. I went to Dr. Victory. I got a prescription for Premarin 2.5, the pills, the little purple pills. And I waited until my insurance kicked in in the middle of May at my job, and I went out and I bought my first bottle of hormones. I was just so happy. I really wanted to just swallow the whole bottle.
<v Foxxjazell>准确地说是五个月后我才真正开始服用荷尔蒙。我去了洛杉矶免费诊所。我去了Victory医生那里。我得到了一张倍美力（Premarin）2.5的处方，那种紫色的小药片。我等到五月中旬工作保险生效，就出去买了我的第一瓶荷尔蒙。我当时太开心了。我真想把整瓶都吞下去。

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:29.660
<v Cris Beam>Taking hormones, which gives them breast, redistribute fat in a more feminine way, soften the face, are only one part of transitioning. There are all kinds of unwritten rules to keep the girls in check. And mainly, they center round appearance and passability. When someone recognizes you're genetically male, it's called getting "clocked" on the West Coast or "spooked" on the East. And no one ever wants that to happen.
<v Cris Beam>服用荷尔蒙，它可以让她们长出乳房，以更女性化的方式重新分配脂肪，柔化面部，这只是转变的一部分。有各种各样的不成文规定来约束女孩们。主要是围绕外表和通关率。在西海岸，当别人认出你是基因上的男性时，这被称为“被抓包”（clocked），在东海岸被称为“惊吓”（spooked）。没有人希望发生这种事。

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:55.410
<v Cris Beam>One girl I talked with described having some guy at a restaurant taunt, "Hey, dude" as she passed by. And it was so traumatizing it kept her from dressing for months afterwards. So the girls are always monitoring each other, keeping an eye out for bad makeup or fake looking boobs, and showering praise on anything fishy. Being fishy is the highest compliment you can get. It means you look like you were born that way. That you were born a girl.
<v Cris Beam>我交谈过的一个女孩描述说，在餐馆里被某个男人嘲笑“嘿，哥们儿”，这让她非常受创，以至于之后几个月都不敢打扮。所以女孩们总是互相监督，留意糟糕的妆容或看起来很假的乳房，并对任何“像样”（fishy）的东西大加赞赏。“像样”是你能得到的最高赞美。这意味着你看起来就像天生如此。就像你天生就是个女孩。

00:11:55.410 --> 00:12:03.740
<v Foxxjazell>It starts with the voice. If you really don't have a fishy voice that can really just destroy the whole image.
<v Foxxjazell>这始于声音。如果你真的没有一个像样的声音，那真的会毁了整个形象。

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:12.020
<v Foxxjazell>A lot of times you can have all the makeup in the world, but if you look like a brick, or you're just not passable, I mean--
<v Foxxjazell>很多时候，你可以有世界上所有的化妆品，但如果你看起来像块砖头，或者就是不合格，我的意思是——

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:14.060
<v Cris Beam>What does it mean to look like a "brick?"
<v Cris Beam>看起来像“砖头”是什么意思？

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:29.150
<v Foxxjazell>To look like a brick, that's a term that a lot of girls use as far as a man in a dress. It means that you may have on a dress and makeup, but yet it's so obvious that you're a guy.
<v Foxxjazell>看起来像砖头，这是很多女孩形容穿裙子的男人的术语。这意味着你可能穿着裙子化着妆，但很明显你是个男人。

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:33.110
<v Cris Beam>Do they say that to each other? Do they call each other that?
<v Cris Beam>她们会这样说对方吗？她们互相这样称呼吗？

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:46.560
<v Foxxjazell>Yeah. A lot of them will say it behind their face and stuff like that. "This dame looks like a brick! This dame, she's bricking it up!" But that's how a lot of girls say that.
