00:21:36.460 --> 00:22:11.590
<v Ariel>She was sitting in the ocean. She wanted to be a human being. And at the end, she turned into a girl and she just looked like a girl. And she never got clocked. And then she went for what she wanted and she did what she wanted. At the end she was happy. Everyone's happy. Her story is just-- I don't know. It'll be like my mom telling me I can be a girl and you can do this. Like that.
<v Ariel>她坐在海洋里。她想成为一个人。最后，她变成了一个女孩，看起来就像个女孩。而且她从来没被抓包。她追求她想要的，做了她想做的。最后她很幸福。每个人都很幸福。她的故事就是——我不知道。就像我妈妈告诉我我可以成为一个女孩，你可以做到这一点。就像那样。

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:16.380
<v Cris Beam>Do you think your mom ever would say it's OK to be a girl?
<v Cris Beam>你觉得你妈妈会说成为女孩没问题吗？

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:22.470
<v Ariel>No.
<v Ariel>不会。

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:42.530
<v Cris Beam>Foxx thinks there's no way Ariel's retirement is going to last. She remembers her own teenage-hood and how she would vow to give up dressing as a woman, but she could never help coming back.
<v Cris Beam>Foxx认为Ariel的退休计划根本持续不了。她记得自己的青少年时期，她是多么发誓要放弃打扮成女人，但她总是忍不住回来。

00:22:42.530 --> 00:22:46.260
<v Foxxjazell>Today was-- I don't know if it was the rain. But it was just a depressing day for me.
<v Foxxjazell>今天——我不知道是不是因为下雨。但对我来说，这是令人沮丧的一天。

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:55.770
<v Cris Beam>A few days after the retirement party, I met up with Foxx again at her apartment. We drank her Kool-Aid from plastic cups and sat on her secondhand couch with the shades drawn.
<v Cris Beam>退休派对后几天，我又在Foxx的公寓见到了她。我们用塑料杯喝着她做的酷爱饮料，拉着窗帘坐在她的二手沙发上。

00:22:55.770 --> 00:23:13.610
<v Foxxjazell>Everything just seemed to be coming together now. But I still have this sense of emptiness inside because apparently Valentine's Day is coming up. Plus my birthday's going to be right after that. And it doesn't make any things much better that I haven't had my first relationship yet.
<v Foxxjazell>一切似乎都走上了正轨。但我内心仍然有一种空虚感，因为情人节显然快到了。再加上我的生日就在那之后。而且我还没有开始我的第一次恋情，这并没有让事情变得更好。

00:23:13.610 --> 00:23:30.700
<v Cris Beam>Whenever I see Foxx she talks obsessively about finding a boyfriend. She's visited psychics and grilled all her friends for leads and can't figure out why nothing has panned out. As she's gotten older, transitioning into a woman is no longer the central drama of her life. Getting a boyfriend is.
<v Cris Beam>每当我见到Foxx，她总是痴迷地谈论寻找男朋友。她拜访过灵媒，盘问过所有的朋友寻找线索，无法理解为什么什么都没有进展。随着年龄增长，转变为女性不再是她生活的核心戏剧。找个男朋友才是。

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:42.050
<v Cris Beam>Today's story is typical. She tells me about a guy down the street who introduced himself as Christopher Columbus. He told her she looked good and they exchanged phone numbers, but he never ended up calling her.
<v Cris Beam>今天的故事很典型。她告诉我街上有一个男人，他自我介绍叫哥伦布。他告诉她她看起来不错，他们交换了电话号码，但他最终没给她打过电话。

00:23:42.050 --> 00:24:22.810
<v Foxxjazell>It was probably, at least a month after that, he still didn't call. And I was walking on that same block and he was out with a bunch of his friends. And one of his friends I think wanted to talk to me. He said, hey, what's up? And the guy that identified himself as Christopher Columbus, he came out of nowhere and said, that's a man. That's a man. You're a guy. You're a guy and you're ugly. You're ugly. And I just walked away. And my heart was just so broken. And like now, every time I pass by his house and everything he's standing outside with his friends or something. He still teases me and say things like that.
<v Foxxjazell>大概在那之后至少一个月，他还是没打。我走在同一条街上，他正和一群朋友在一起。他的一位朋友我想大概是想和我说话。他说，嘿，你好吗？那个自称哥伦布的男人突然冲出来说，那是个男人。那是个人妖。你是个男人。你是个男人，你真丑。你真丑。我就走开了。我的心碎了。现在，每次我经过他家，或者他站在外面和朋友们在一起时。他还会嘲笑我，说那种话。

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:42.240
<v Foxxjazell>The last time he talked to me was probably like three weeks ago. And he asked me, let me ask you something. Have you had it cut it off yet? And he was referring to have I had the surgery yet. And I said, that's personal. And he said, well, I guess that means that you haven't had it cut off. And I just walked away.
<v Foxxjazell>他上次跟我说话大概是三周前。他问我，让我问你点事。你把那东西切掉了吗？他指的是我有没有做过手术。我说，那是私事。他说，好吧，我想那意味着你还没切掉。我就走开了。

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:46.290
<v Cris Beam>How do you handle that kind of sadness and that kind of rejection?
<v Cris Beam>你如何处理那种悲伤和那种拒绝？

00:24:46.290 --> 00:25:01.320
<v Foxxjazell>Well, it's been the inspiration for five songs that I've written.
<v Foxxjazell>好吧，这成为了我写的五首歌的灵感来源。

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:12.540
<v Cris Beam>Foxx wants to be a huge star. She's a singer and a rapper and she spends all of her money on studio time, recording songs about coming out, and being transgender, and wanting the world to understand.
<v Cris Beam>Foxx想成为大明星。她是一名歌手兼说唱歌手，她把所有的钱都花在录音室时间上，录制关于出柜、成为变性人、想要世界理解的歌曲。

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:28.010
<v Cris Beam>She imagines being somewhat of a sex symbol too, and knows her future boyfriend will have to put up with that. She constantly worries about when that boyfriend will come.
<v Cris Beam>她也想象自己成为某种性感偶像，并知道未来的男朋友必须忍受这一点。她不断地担心那个男朋友什么时候会来。

00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:41.300
<v Cris Beam>It was a few weeks after her 21st birthday that Foxx called me up all excited. It had finally happened, she gushed. Everything she dreamed of. She told me she had met a man. Actually, they met a week ago and he was now living in her apartment.
<v Cris Beam>在Foxx 21岁生日几周后，她兴奋地给我打电话。终于发生了，她滔滔不绝地说。她梦想的一切。她告诉我她遇到了一个男人。实际上，他们一周前认识的，现在他已经搬进她的公寓了。

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:58.760
<v Foxxjazell>It's really exciting because I mean, it's like last week I'm single and now this week I have a boyfriend and he's cooking for me. And I'm falling in love with him and he's falling in love with me. It's just happening so fast. It's like a dream.
<v Foxxjazell>这真的很令人兴奋，因为我的意思是，上周我还是单身，现在这周我就有了男朋友，他还在为我做饭。我爱上了他，他也爱上了我。一切发生得太快了。就像做梦一样。

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:19.120
<v Cris Beam>If there's any question in your mind at all about straight guys going for transgender girls, remember, these girls are fantastically adorable. Foxx's new boyfriend, Sergio, is 23. He's a Puerto Rican guy from New York with an ex-wife and a daughter. Foxx is the first transgender woman he's ever dated. He's straight and sweet and totally in love.
<v Cris Beam>如果你的脑海中对异性恋男人追求变性女孩有任何疑问，记住，这些女孩非常可爱。Foxx的新男友Sergio 23岁。他是个来自纽约的波多黎各人，有个前妻和一个女儿。Foxx是他交往过的第一个变性女性。他是异性恋，很贴心，完全陷入了爱河。

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:31.440
<v Foxxjazell>I even tell them it's like, I don't believe you some time because of the fact that you're so fine and you can get any real girl that you want to. But yet, you're here with me. And I just want to pinch myself and just wake up.
<v Foxxjazell>我甚至告诉他们，我有的时候不相信你，因为你这么出色，你可以得到任何你想要的真正的女孩。但你却在这里和我在一起。我真想捏自己一把，然后醒过来。

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:35.570
<v Cris Beam>How do you feel when she says that you could get any real girl? What does that do for you?
<v Cris Beam>当她说你可以得到任何真正的女孩时，你感觉如何？这对你有什么影响？

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:52.450
<v Sergio>Well, I actually said that maybe she's true about that, but my feeling is I can never find someone like her. And I think I'm going to be all right after this.
<v Sergio>嗯，我实际上说也许她说得对，但我感觉我永远找不到像她这样的人了。而且我想在这之后我会没事的。

00:26:52.450 --> 00:27:04.340
<v Foxxjazell>I wanted to comment about something. Sergio was listening to my song, "If I was a Real Girl" and he made a comment that just struck me. And I forgot what he said. What did you say, baby? When you listened to my song.
<v Foxxjazell>我想评论一下某件事。Sergio当时正在听我的歌《如果我是个真正的女孩》，他发表了一句让我印象深刻的评论。我忘了他说什么了。你说什么了，宝贝？当你听我的歌时。

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:36.590
<v Sergio>Oh, I say, you don't have to be a real woman to somebody like me who cares and love you. I don't see what she has outside, I see what is inside of her. That's the point. That's the view that I see about all this thing. Like I always say, the heart doesn't see, only feels.
<v Sergio>哦，我说，对于像我这样关心和爱你的人来说，你不需要成为一个真正的女人。我不在乎她外面有什么，我在乎她内在有什么。这就是重点。这就是我对这一切的看法。就像我常说的，心不看，只感受。

00:27:36.590 --> 00:27:55.980
<v Cris Beam>When I tell people about this book project they all want to know which of the kids are really women or really men. By which they mean, who has had the genital surgery. But the surgery is irrelevant. Lots of trans men never have it because it costs $70,000. And plenty of trans women, like Foxx, don't want it.
<v Cris Beam>当我告诉人们这个书稿项目时，他们都想知道哪些孩子是真正的女性或真正的男性。他们的意思是，谁做了生殖器手术。但手术是不相关的。很多变性男性从来没做过，因为它要花7万美元。而且很多变性女性，像Foxx，也不想要。

00:27:55.980 --> 00:28:24.520
<v Cris Beam>It's funny, feminism has worked so hard to prove that women are more than what's in their pants. But when it comes to transsexuals, it's all people can think about. So I counter crudeness with crudeness and ask them, if you somehow got into an accident and lost everything below the waist, would you be any less woman, any less man? If you think you're a woman you are one. We're not used to that idea. We're not used to believing that gender is determined only by our hearts.
<v Cris Beam>很有趣，女权主义为了证明女性不仅仅是裤子里的东西而努力工作。但当谈到变性人时，这就是人们唯一能想到的东西。所以我以粗俗对付粗俗，问他们，如果你不知怎么出了事故，腰部以下什么都没了，你还会少一点女人味，少一点男人味吗？如果你认为自己是女人，你就是。我们不习惯那种想法。我们不习惯相信性别仅仅由我们的内心决定。

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:29.880
<v Cris Beam>In Ariel's case, she couldn't suppress this heart and her retirement turned out to be more of a pause then a real break.
<v Cris Beam>在Ariel的例子中，她无法压抑这颗心，她的退休与其说是一个真正的结束，不如说更像是一个暂停。

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:40.510
<v Cris Beam>So the last time that I saw you, you were deciding to retire and stop dressing. And how long were you going to take your retirement for?
<v Cris Beam>上次我见到你时，你决定退休并停止打扮。你打算把退休计划持续多久？

00:28:40.510 --> 00:28:42.350
<v Ariel>For a month.
<v Ariel>一个月。

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:45.020
<v Cris Beam>And how long did it last?
<v Cris Beam>结果持续了多久？

00:28:45.020 --> 00:28:48.570
<v Ariel>It lasted for like a week.
<v Ariel>大概持续了一周。

00:28:48.570 --> 00:28:59.470
<v Cris Beam>A few days after her retirement party, Ariel's friends started calling her, asking her to go out, and dress up, and take pictures with them. So she went and it felt great. Though it scares her that if felt great.
<v Cris Beam>退休派对后几天，Ariel的朋友们开始给她打电话，约她出去，一起打扮，一起拍照。所以她去了，感觉棒极了。尽管这让她害怕，因为感觉真的棒极了。

00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:08.680
<v Ariel>I failed. I did fail. It's like I'm going to go to hell. That's what I think in my head.
<v Ariel>我失败了。我确实失败了。就像我要下地狱一样。我在脑子里就是这么想的。

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:10.590
<v Cris Beam>Why would God disapprove?
<v Cris Beam>上帝为什么要反对？

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:24.050
<v Ariel>Because it's in the Bible that you were born a man and a man you are.
<v Ariel>因为圣经里说你天生是个男人，你就是一个男人。

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:33.820
<v Cris Beam>Did you learn anything from your retirement? Did you learn how to golf? Did you learn anything from your retirement?
<v Cris Beam>你从退休中学会了什么吗？你学会打高尔夫了吗？你从退休中学到了什么吗？

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:50.470
<v Ariel>Yes, that I'm a boy. I'm a woman.
<v Ariel>是的，我是个男孩。我是个女人。

00:29:50.470 --> 00:30:14.780
<v Cris Beam>Ariel is struggling back and forth, dressing some days as a boy and some days as a girl. But guiltily. Ariel as a boy is withdrawn, blinking vacantly and drowning in baggy, hide-everything jeans. He almost disappears. But as a girl, Ariel is vibrant, believable, sassy. Claiming space with her walk and challenging you to meet her eye.
<v Cris Beam>Ariel在来回挣扎，有时候打扮成男孩，有时候打扮成女孩。但心怀愧疚。作为男孩的Ariel很退缩，眼神空洞，淹没在宽松的、遮住一切的牛仔裤里。他几乎消失了。但作为女孩，Ariel充满活力、真实、时髦。她用走路的姿态占据空间，并挑战你与她对视。

00:30:14.780 --> 00:30:30.850
<v Cris Beam>When Ariel was going to a rough inner city school a few years back she tried to be tough and masculine. But the boy's still harassed her for acting too girlie and she had to drop out. To get even, she showed up one last time dressed up, as she says, hoochie, in a tiny dress and heels.
<v Cris Beam>几年前，当Ariel在一所粗暴的内城学校上学时，她试图表现得强硬和阳刚。但男孩们仍然因为她表现得太娘而骚扰她，她不得不退学。为了报复，她最后一次打扮得，正如她所说的，风骚，穿着小裙子和高跟鞋出现了。

00:30:30.850 --> 00:30:47.120
<v Cris Beam>The boys didn't recognize her as she walked around the quad. And some even asked for her number. I love this defiance, this revenge in the face of such pain.
<v Cris Beam>她在校园里走动时，男孩们没认出她。有些人甚至问她要电话号码。我喜欢这种反抗，在面对如此痛苦时的这种复仇。

00:30:47.120 --> 00:31:15.440
<v Cris Beam>Ariel recently had to leave her sister's house and south central neighborhood because neighbor boys, once again, we're threatening to kill her for acting too gay. She's now living across town in her own apartment with an older transgender woman. Ariel's still not dressing full time, but the other day she did call me up to see if I would take her out for her 19th birthday. She wanted me to buy her a wig.
<v Cris Beam>Ariel最近不得不离开她姐姐的房子和南中央区的社区，因为邻居男孩们又一次威胁说要杀了她，因为她表现得太像同性恋。她现在住在大街另一头，和一位年长的变性女性一起住在自己的公寓里。Ariel仍然不是全职打扮，但前几天她确实打电话给我，看我是否愿意带她出去过19岁生日。她想让我给她买顶假发。

00:31:15.440 --> 00:31:31.760
<v Ira Glass>Cris Beam is a writer living in Los Angeles. These days, Foxxjazell has a day job doing street outreach to transgender youth. To hear her music you can download MP3 files from the This American Life website, www.thisamericanlife.org.
<v Ira Glass>Cris Beam是住在洛杉矶的一名作家。如今，Foxxjazell有一份白天的工作，从事针对变性青少年的街头外展工作。要听她的音乐，你可以从《美国生活》网站下载MP3文件，www.thisamericanlife.org。

00:31:31.760 --> 00:32:47.840
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, somebody famous you've never heard of. Somebody who wanted a job you probably don't want. And the well-known catchphrase, "this is my boomstick." You know, this is my boomstick. In a minute from Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，一个你从未听说过的名人。一个想要一份你可能不想要的工作的人。还有那句著名的口头禅，“这是我的雷管枪”。你知道，这是我的雷管枪。国际公共广播电台的节目稍后继续。

00:32:47.840 --> 00:33:03.710
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life , I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, Living the Dream. People who decided to remake their lives around some new vision of themselves. We have arrived at act two of our program.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》，我是Ira Glass。当然，我们每周在节目中选择一个主题，为您带来各种各样的故事。今天的节目，生活梦想。那些决定围绕自己对生活的新愿景来重塑人生的人。我们已经到达了节目的第二幕。

00:33:03.710 --> 00:33:13.340
<v Ira Glass>Act two, Agent to the Stars. John Hodgman tells this story of a dream that was a modest dream. Modest enough that it could actually come true.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，明星经纪人。John Hodgman讲述了这个关于一个适度梦想的故事。适度到它真的能实现。

00:33:13.340 --> 00:33:27.910
<v John Hodgman>If there's one thing you learn the day you start working in publishing, it's that everyone wants to write a book. No matter how lowly your position, the moment you get that first job you can be sure that 18 of your father's friends will soon be calling you about how to get their spy novel published.
<v John Hodgman>如果你在出版业开始工作的第一天学到了一件事，那就是每个人都想写书。无论你的职位多么卑微，当你得到第一份工作的那一刻，你可以肯定，父亲的18个朋友很快会打电话给你，询问如何出版他们的谍战小说。

00:33:27.910 --> 00:34:06.420
<v John Hodgman>There's something so alluring about becoming an author, and it's something so daunting and enigmatic about the process of getting published that even the most accomplished men and women, world travelers, captains of industry, cower before it. This is how the greenest publishing recruits end up editing the inner musings of gangsters, telling millionaires to kill their darlings, spell checking presidents. And this is how, with little training, less experience, and only a vague idea of what I was doing, I became the professional literary agent for the world famous movie star Mr. Bruce Campbell.
<v John Hodgman>成为一名作家有着某种诱人的东西，而出版的过程又如此令人望而生畏且神秘，以至于即使是成就最卓著的男女，世界旅行者，工业巨头，也会在它面前退缩。这就是为什么最新鲜的出版新兵最终编辑黑帮的内心独白，告诉百万富翁要割舍他们的至爱，校对总统的拼写。这也是为什么，在几乎没有培训、经验更少，只有模糊想法的情况下，我成为了世界著名电影明星Bruce Campbell先生的职业文学代理人。

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:37.140
<v John Hodgman>You don't know who Bruce Campbell is. That's fine. As his agent, I've had to explain it a lot. Bruce Campbell is an actor. He was Ellen's boss on Ellen , Jack on Jack of All Trades , Autolycus, King of Thieves on Xena: Warrior Princess . In the movies Maniac Cop 1 and Maniac Cop 2 he played the cop who was not a maniac. He's the sort of actor who dies in the first 10 minutes of the movie Congo . Which he did.
<v John Hodgman>你不知道Bruce Campbell是谁。没关系。作为他的代理人，我不得不解释很多次。Bruce Campbell是一个演员。他曾是《艾伦》中艾伦的老板，《杰克全才》中的杰克，《齐娜：战士公主》中的“盗贼之王”Autolycus。在电影《鬼警探1》和《鬼警探2》中，他扮演了那个不是疯子的警察。他是那种在电影《刚果惊魂》前10分钟就死掉的演员。他确实死在那里。

00:34:37.140 --> 00:34:55.690
<v John Hodgman>The music you're listening to right now is the theme from The Adventures of Briscoe County Jr. in which Bruce Campbell played a cowboy. It was canceled after one season. We still use the theme song though, from time to time, to introduce sporting events, like the World Series or the Olympics.
<v John Hodgman>你现在听到的音乐是《布里斯科县大冒险》的主题曲，Bruce Campbell在其中饰演一名牛仔。它只播了一季就被取消了。不过，我们还是会不时使用这首主题曲来介绍体育赛事，比如世界大赛或奥运会。

00:34:55.690 --> 00:35:25.740
<v John Hodgman>If none of this rings a bell, perhaps you've seen a movie called The Evil Dead or one of its two sequels. In Evil Dead II: Dead by Dawn there's a character named Ash, whose hand is infested by an evil spirit. After the possessed hand slaps him all over the house, he cuts it off with a chainsaw. But it still isn't dead. Ash goes out to the work shed and what follows is one of the great parodies ever to be filmed of the classic hero gets ready to kick-ass montage.
<v John Hodgman>如果这些都没让你有印象，也许你见过一部叫《鬼玩人》的电影或者它的两部续集。《鬼玩人2：死亡之舞》里有一个叫Ash的角色，他的手被邪灵感染了。在那只被附身的手把他整屋子扇耳光之后，他用电锯把它切断了。但它还是没死。Ash走到工具棚，接着发生的是电影史上最伟大的模仿动作之一——英雄准备大干一场的蒙太奇。

00:35:25.740 --> 00:35:51.960
<v John Hodgman>Close up of Ash gathering his tools and ammunition, modifying the chain saw. Which he then straps on to the stump where his hand used to be. Pulling the rip chord, he uses it to saw the end of a double barreled shotgun. He twirls the gun cowboy style around his trigger finger, slips it into a holster on his back. As the chainsaw's engine roars, he stares into the camera and deadpans the immortal line--
<v John Hodgman>特写镜头展示Ash收集工具和弹药，改造电锯。然后他把它绑在曾经是手的地方。拉动启动绳，他用它锯断了一把双管猎枪的枪管。他像牛仔一样用食指转动枪支，把它插进背后的皮套里。当电锯引擎咆哮时，他盯着镜头，死板地说了那句不朽的台词——

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:53.700
<v Bruce Campbell>Groovy.
<v Bruce Campbell>Groovy（酷毙了）。

00:35:53.700 --> 00:36:24.120
<v John Hodgman>That's Bruce Campbell. And with that line, a B-movie star was born. Because if you don't know who Bruce Campbell is there's a growing circle of devoted fans who do, and they want you to meet him. They build websites in his honor. They make refrigerator magnets with his picture on them and they sell them on e-Bay. When Napster was on its last legs this past spring, the only thing you could be sure to find there were German drinking songs and Bruce Campbell's most famous lines. "Groovy." "This is my boomstick." "Hail to the King, baby."
<v John Hodgman>那就是Bruce Campbell。那句台词诞生了一位B级片明星。因为如果你不知道Bruce Campbell是谁，有一个不断扩大的忠实粉丝圈知道，他们想让你见到他。他们为他建立网站。他们制作印有他照片的冰箱贴，并在eBay上出售。当Napster在去年春天行将就木时，你唯一能确定找到的就是德国饮酒歌和Bruce Campbell最著名的台词。“Groovy。”“这是我的雷管枪。”“向国王致敬，宝贝。”

00:36:24.120 --> 00:36:37.000
<v Bruce Campbell>Hail to the King, baby. Good. Bad. I'm the guy with the gun. Give me some sugar, baby. I got news for you, pal. You ain't leading but two things right now, Jack and [BLEEP]. And Jack left town.
<v Bruce Campbell>向国王致敬，宝贝。好的。坏的。我就是那个拿着枪的家伙。给我点糖果，宝贝。我有新闻告诉你，伙计。你现在除了两样东西什么也领导不了，杰克和[哔]。而杰克离开了镇子。

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:48.460
<v John Hodgman>On their own or dropped over homemade techno music, they're the passwords into the inner circle of the cult of Bruce Campbell.
<v John Hodgman>无论是自制还是混音在自制的高科技音乐里，它们都是进入Bruce Campbell邪教崇拜核心圈的密码。

00:36:48.460 --> 00:37:21.080
<v John Hodgman>The cult is nearly equally men and women, perhaps because Bruce's charm fall somewhere between Cary Grant and the Three Stooges. And maybe it's because he's not very well known that you end up feeling that he's your own personal celebrity that no one else knows about. Even if you're aware that Bruce Campbell has a small army of avid fans, there's something about him that makes you think that if you ever had the chance to meet him, the two of you would definitely hang out. You might even watch Bruce Campbell movies together.
<v John Hodgman>这个粉丝圈男女比例几乎均等，也许是因为Bruce的魅力介于Cary Grant和“三个臭皮匠”之间。也许正是因为他不太出名，你最终会觉得他是你自己专属的、没人知道的明星。即使你知道Bruce Campbell有一小支狂热粉丝军团，他身上有什么东西让你觉得如果你有机会见到他，你们绝对会混在一起。你们甚至可能会一起看Bruce Campbell的电影。

00:37:21.080 --> 00:37:45.280
<v John Hodgman>That was my line of thinking anyway, in early 1997. I was a young, literary agent who had never sold a book, and a Bruce Campbell fan. That spring, my office finally was hooked up to something I had only heard whispers of called the World Wide Web. It didn't do much for office productivity, but on that first day I typed in the words Bruce and Campbell and it changed my life forever.
<v John Hodgman>无论如何，那是我在1997年初的想法。我是一名年轻的文学代理人，从没卖过一本书，也是Bruce Campbell的粉丝。那年春天，我的办公室终于连接上了我只听说过传闻的东西，叫万维网。它对办公室生产力没多大帮助，但在第一天，我输入了Bruce和Campbell这两个词，它永远改变了我的生活。

00:37:45.280 --> 00:38:09.140
<v John Hodgman>It turned out that Bruce Campbell had his own website. And by his own, I mean his own. Not some slick corporate product, but a homemade looking affair maintained by volunteers, and featuring long essays from Bruce, advice to aspiring actors, notes on how much orange juice costs at the Cannes Film Festival. Reports from the set of McHale's Navy , a terrible Tom Arnold movie that no human would ever see.
<v John Hodgman>事实证明Bruce Campbell有自己的网站。我说的“自己的”，是指他自己的。不是什么精美的企业产品，而是志愿者维护的自制网页，以Bruce的长篇散文、给想当演员的人的建议、关于戛纳电影节橙汁价格的笔记为特色。甚至还有《麦克海尔的海军》的片场报告，那是一部没有人会看的糟糕的Tom Arnold电影。

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:19.430
<v John Hodgman>There was a link to an email address and I clicked on it and I stared at the empty form. I didn't know what to say. But then, of course, I did.
<v John Hodgman>有一个电子邮件地址的链接，我点击了它，盯着那个空白表格。我不知道说什么。但当然，我还是写了。

00:38:19.430 --> 00:38:35.570
<v John Hodgman>I'm not only a fan, I wrote. I am also a professional literary agent. Have you ever thought about writing a book?
<v John Hodgman>我不仅是粉丝，我写道。我还是一名职业文学代理人。你有没有想过写本书？

00:38:35.570 --> 00:39:00.190
<v John Hodgman>I didn't really expect him to write back. It certainly never occurred to me that this book nonsense would be something I'd ever have to think about again in my life. But after all, one man's B-movie god is another man's lonely working stiff factor stuck in New Zealand filming Xena: Warrior Princess , taking digital pictures of the strange southern hemisphere electrical outlets and posting them on the internet. He must've been bored out of his mind.
<v John Hodgman>我没真的指望他回信。我当然也没想到这种写书的废话会是我一生中需要再次考虑的事情。但毕竟，一个人的B级片之神是另一个人的孤独打工仔，被困在新西兰拍摄《齐娜：战士公主》，拍着奇怪的南半球电源插座的数码照片并发布到网上。他一定无聊透顶了。

00:39:00.190 --> 00:39:14.420
<v John Hodgman>So the next thing I know I'm chatting with my hero on a crackly overseas connection about why the world must hear the confessions of a B-movie actor.
<v John Hodgman>接下来的事情我知道了，我在裂纹不断的海外连线中和我的英雄聊天，谈论为什么世界必须听到B级片演员的自白。

00:39:14.420 --> 00:39:25.670
<v John Hodgman>People have celebrity dreams all the time. David Letterman moves into your apartment building. The Pope calls wanting to know if you need him to cat sit. But this phone call was the real thing.
<v John Hodgman>人们总是有名人梦。大卫·莱特曼搬进你的公寓楼。教皇打电话来问你需不需要他帮你照顾猫。但这个电话是真实的。

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:45.380
<v John Hodgman>There I was telling him how a book proposal is written, what an editor does, and on the other end of the line it's Bruce Campbell saying, "Uh huh, I see. A proposal you say." When he actually said groovy, it was very unsettling.
<v John Hodgman>我当时告诉他书稿建议书是怎么写的，编辑是做什么的，而在电话那头，Bruce Campbell在说，“嗯哼，我明白。你说建议书？”当他真的说出“groovy”时，真是让人心神不宁。

00:39:45.380 --> 00:40:16.670
<v John Hodgman>I wanted to draw our conversation out in part because I thought I would probably never speak to him again, but I also wanted it to end as quickly as possible so I could put it behind me, make it into a story, get back to my normal life. To do this I even tried to discourage him. You should probably know I said, I've never sold a book before. But when we hung up, it wasn't over. I wouldn't go back to my normal life for a long time. Somehow, I don't remember how, I had sold him on me.
<v John Hodgman>我想延长我们的谈话，部分是因为我觉得我可能再也不会跟他说话了，但也因为我想让它尽快结束，这样我就可以把它抛在脑后，把它变成一个故事，回到我正常的生活中。为此我甚至试图劝阻他。你应该知道，我说，我以前从未卖过书。但当我们挂断电话时，并没有结束。在很长一段时间里，我不会回到正常的生活中。不知怎么地，我不记得是怎么做到的，我让他认可了我。

00:40:16.670 --> 00:40:37.100
<v John Hodgman>After that, it was all downhill. And for a reason that I was too much of a fan to anticipate. The nature of B-movie stardom is such that you can be a celebrity on one hand and a nobody on the other. In a hotel that's hosting a horror movie expo, Bruce Campbell may not be able to get arrested in the lobby, but in the conventional halls he's a living god.
<v John Hodgman>在那之后，一切都走下坡路了。原因是我太粉丝了，没能预料到。B级片明星的本质在于，你一方面可以是名人，另一方面可以是无名小卒。在举办恐怖片博览会的酒店里，Bruce Campbell在大堂里可能连被逮捕的机会都没有，但在会议大厅里，他是活着的上帝。

00:40:37.100 --> 00:40:57.450
<v John Hodgman>Turns out though, that most of the editors I sent his proposal to don't live in the conventional hall, they live in the lobby. If they were aware at all of the world in which Bruce is a star, it was only to take the time to sneer. Even those who were closet Bruce fans like me, were either unable or unwilling to take a stand and let their geek flags fly.
<v John Hodgman>然而事实证明，我寄出他建议书的大多数编辑并不住在会议大厅，他们住在酒店大堂。如果他们对Bruce是明星的世界有一点了解，那也只是为了嘲笑一下。即使是像我一样躲在柜子里的Bruce粉丝，要么没有能力，要么不愿意站出来，展现他们的极客风采。

00:40:57.450 --> 00:41:25.980
<v John Hodgman>So this project, which initially seemed to spell easy money for both of us, gradually became a painful exercise in enduring rejection. Which put me in a complicated position. I had achieved my dream the moment Bruce first called me. The rest was gravy. But now, against all common sense, Bruce was entrusting me with his dream and I was letting him down.
<v John Hodgman>所以这个项目，起初似乎对我们双方来说都是轻松挣钱的项目，逐渐变成了忍受拒绝的痛苦练习。这让我处于一个复杂的境地。在Bruce第一次给我打电话的那一刻，我就实现了梦想。剩下的都是额外收获。但现在，违背一切常识，Bruce把他的梦想托付给了我，而我却让他失望了。

00:41:25.980 --> 00:41:49.710
<v John Hodgman>It was time to bring out the big guns. Or, as we say in publishing, it was time to bring the pope over to cat sit. Bruce had to put himself in front of those few remaining editors who hadn't passed. Make them feel as giddy as I had during that first phone call. He had to come to New York.
<v John Hodgman>是时候拿出杀手锏了。或者，正如我们在出版界所说的，是时候让教皇来照顾猫了。Bruce必须亲自面对那些还没拒绝的少数编辑。让他们感到我第一次通话时那样的眩晕。他必须来纽约。

00:41:49.710 --> 00:42:09.590
<v John Hodgman>I had obviously always wanted to meet Bruce in person, but not like this. Not when the sole reason for his coming was because I had to date, been failing miserably. So I tried to distract him with a quick tour of my office. We reached my cubicle at the top of the stairs. That's me, I said, with a quick wave to my sad, little chair. Let's go to dinner now.
<v John Hodgman>我当然一直想亲眼见到Bruce，但不是这样。不是当他来的唯一原因是因为我到目前为止失败得一塌糊涂时。所以我试图通过一个简短的办公室参观来转移他的注意力。我们到了楼梯顶端的隔间。那是我，我说，快速向我悲伤的小椅子挥手。我们现在去吃饭吧。

00:42:09.590 --> 00:43:01.920
<v John Hodgman>As we walked down Sixth avenue, my girlfriend and Bruce's wife a little ahead of us, Bruce and I painfully tried to make small talk. At dinner, I finally got the experience every Bruce Campbell fan dreams about. I was hanging out with Bruce. Specifically, I was eating hummus with him. Does that sound exciting? Of course it doesn't. Everything was normal, completely un-dreamlike. And that's what made it great. We were two couples having dinner, talking about the books we had read. The only thing that skewed the night slightly towards surreality was my shaking need for a cigarette. I was holding off. I thought it might offend him. But even after, when Bruce and his wife caught me and my girlfriend frantically lighting up on the street, it wasn't weird. We all had a laugh about it.
<v John Hodgman>当我们沿着第六大道走的时候，我的女朋友和Bruce的妻子在我们前面一点，Bruce和我都痛苦地试图寻找话题。在晚餐时，我终于得到了每个Bruce Campbell粉丝梦想的体验。我和Bruce混在一起。具体来说，我在和他吃鹰嘴豆泥。这听起来刺激吗？当然不。一切都很正常，完全不像是梦境。而这正是让它变得伟大的原因。我们两对夫妇在吃晚饭，谈论我们读过的书。唯一让夜晚稍微偏向超现实的是我颤抖着对香烟的需求。我忍住了。我想这可能会冒犯他。但甚至在那之后，当Bruce和他的妻子在街上看到我和我的女朋友疯狂点烟时，并不奇怪。我们都为此大笑起来。

00:43:01.920 --> 00:43:22.690
<v John Hodgman>The book finally sold tot he last editor we met that day. The one who had brought in the Bruce Campbell issue of Fangoria magazine for an autograph before introducing Bruce around to his bosses. Actually, he didn't even buy it at that house. He had to quit first and take a job at another company, and then beg his boss before he could even make a modest offer. We quickly accepted.
<v John Hodgman>这本书终于卖给了那天我们见的最后一位编辑。那个在把Bruce介绍给他的老板之前，把一本《Fangoria》杂志的Bruce Campbell专刊拿去签名的人。实际上，他甚至没在那家公司买下它。他必须先辞职，去另一家公司找份工作，然后乞求他的老板，才能做出一个适度的报价。我们很快接受了。

00:43:22.690 --> 00:43:44.550
<v John Hodgman>It was a huge weight off my shoulders. It's not usual for a book to take almost two years to sell. Typically, it's more like two weeks. I was hoping that Bruce didn't know this as keenly as I did, but I could never ask him. If you're someone's literary agent, the one piece of advice you can't give them is, go get a good literary agent.
<v John Hodgman>这减轻了我肩上的巨大负担。一本书卖出几乎需要两年是不常见的。通常情况下，大约两周。我希望Bruce没有像我这样敏锐地意识到这一点，但我永远无法问他。如果你是某人的文学代理人，你不能给他们的一条建议是，去找个好的文学代理人。

00:43:44.550 --> 00:44:04.760
<v John Hodgman>The day we sold the book I was on vacation with my girlfriend, whom I'd been dating for 10 years. The next day, I asked her to marry me. We can invite Bruce, I said.
<v John Hodgman>卖书的那天，我正和约会了10年的女朋友度假。第二天，我向她求婚了。我们可以邀请Bruce，我说。

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:06.090
<v John Hodgman>Hey Bruce, how you doing?
<v John Hodgman>嘿Bruce，你好吗？

00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:09.090
<v Bruce Campbell>Greetings. Greetings. Enter. Thank you. I see you've brought goods.
<v Bruce Campbell>问候。问候。进来。谢谢。我看到你带了东西来。

00:44:09.090 --> 00:44:12.560
<v John Hodgman>Yes, I brought a few sandwiches here. This room is pretty nice.
<v John Hodgman>是的，我带了一些三明治。这房间挺好的。

00:44:12.560 --> 00:44:25.170
<v Ira Glass>It's been four years now since that first email and I'm visiting Bruce at The New Yorker Hotel in Manhattan. The book is about to come out and Bruce has come to push it with a Q&A, an autograph signing at the Fangoria Weekend of Horror.
<v Ira Glass>距离那封电子邮件已经四年了，我正在曼哈顿纽约人酒店拜访Bruce。书即将出版，Bruce来到这里参加问答环节，在Fangoria恐怖周末进行签售。

00:44:25.170 --> 00:44:33.360
<v Man 1>Give it up for Bruce Campbell.
<v Man 1>为Bruce Campbell鼓掌。

00:44:33.360 --> 00:44:41.270
<v John Hodgman>I'm not his agent anymore. For various reasons I've left the business, but Bruce and I have stayed in touch. Mainly because I refuse to let him go.
<v John Hodgman>我不再是他的代理人了。出于各种原因，我已经离开了这个行业，但Bruce和我都保持着联系。主要是因为我拒绝让他离开。

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:52.960
<v Bruce Campbell>I want everyone to stand up. Stand up. Every single one of ya. Raise your right hand. Repeat after me. I.
<v Bruce Campbell>我希望大家站起来。站起来。你们每一个。举起右手。跟着我说。我。

00:44:52.960 --> 00:44:53.780
<v Audience>I.
<v Audience>我。

00:44:53.780 --> 00:44:55.230
<v Bruce Campbell>State your name.
<v Bruce Campbell>报上你的名字。

00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:58.630
<v Audience>State your name.
<v Audience>报上你的名字。

00:44:58.630 --> 00:45:01.540
<v Bruce Campbell>Comedians, huh?
<v Bruce Campbell>喜剧演员，是吧？

00:45:01.540 --> 00:45:18.230
<v John Hodgman>Bruce is famous for antagonizing his fans, and they love it. They love him. In this room, Bruce Campbell is the biggest star in the world. His book is the most important book in the world. And I am his esteemed colleague. His former professional literary agent.
<v John Hodgman>Bruce以对抗粉丝而闻名，而他们热爱这一点。他们热爱他。在这个房间里，Bruce Campbell是世界上最伟大的明星。他的书是世界上最重要的书。而我是他受人尊敬的同事。他以前的职业文学代理人。

00:45:18.230 --> 00:45:19.640
<v Bruce Campbell>Be better over here. Yes sir.
<v Bruce Campbell>在这里表现得更好。是的，先生。

00:45:19.640 --> 00:45:22.560
<v Man 2>Are there any plans for Evil Dead IV?
<v Man 2>有关于《鬼玩人4》的计划吗？

00:45:22.560 --> 00:45:27.000
<v Bruce Campbell>God, that's so weird. I've never heard that question before.
<v Bruce Campbell>天哪，那太奇怪了。我以前从未听过这个问题。

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:47.430
<v John Hodgman>As the convention continues, Bruce signs posters, head shots, DVDS, bootleg scripts, bared breasts, anything the fans have to offer. The line goes to the back of the room and out the door. In this crowd, there's almost a paternal sense of pride and even concern. They feel that Bruce has never had the big break he deserves.
<v John Hodgman>随着大会继续，Bruce签署海报、头像、DVD、盗版剧本、裸露的乳房，粉丝们提供的任何东西。队伍排到了房间后部，一直延伸到门口。在这个人群中，几乎有一种父亲般的骄傲甚至关切。他们觉得Bruce从来没有得到他应得的重大突破。

00:45:47.430 --> 00:45:49.940
<v John Hodgman>You're brothers? Are you a Bruce Campbell fan as well?
<v John Hodgman>你是兄弟？你也是Bruce Campbell的粉丝吗？

00:45:49.940 --> 00:45:54.420
<v Man 3>Oh, big time. Yeah. My brother and I, we're like-- he's like our idol, basically.
<v Man 3>哦，超级粉丝。是的。我和我兄弟，我们就像——他基本上是我们的偶像。

00:45:54.420 --> 00:45:55.260
<v John Hodgman>Really?
<v John Hodgman>真的吗？

00:45:55.260 --> 00:46:18.270
<v Man 3>We pretty much grew up watching the horror movies. That's like totally won my brother over with the whole Evil Dead series. In fact, I hope one day when I get really big in the money in the stock markets and everything, I'm going to buy a studio like, for example. And I'm to make him a star, like one of the big movies. Top notch.
<v Man 3>我们基本上是看恐怖片长大的。那真的让我兄弟完全被整个《鬼玩人》系列迷住了。事实上，我希望有一天当我能在股市里赚大钱时，我要买一家工作室，例如。然后我要让他成为明星，比如大电影。顶级水平。

00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:37.480
<v John Hodgman>Right now I'm a freelance writer, but I used to be a literary agent. And in fact, I represented Bruce's book. I sold his book to the publishers. And I'm going this show about how four years ago when I first started talking to him about doing a book, for me I had the same impulse. It was wanting to bring him to a bigger audience. And you're saying how you'd like to--
<v John Hodgman>我现在是一名自由撰稿人，但我以前是文学代理人。事实上，我代理了Bruce的书。我把他的书卖给了出版商。我要做这个节目，讲述四年前我第一次开始和他谈论写书时，对我来说，我有同样的冲动。那就是想要把他带给更多的观众。而你正在说你如何想要——

00:46:37.480 --> 00:46:41.800
<v Man 3>Exactly. That's awesome, man. Way to go. Definitely.
<v Man 3>正是如此。那太棒了，伙计。干得好。绝对。

00:46:41.800 --> 00:46:46.400
<v John Hodgman>I just think it's interesting because obviously, other people feel the same way. That he deserves more exposure.
<v John Hodgman>我只是认为这很有趣，因为显然，其他人也有同样的感觉。他值得更多的曝光。

00:46:46.400 --> 00:46:52.670
<v Man 3>He definitely deserves more. Yes. Definitely. He's really a top notch actor in reality.
<v Man 3>他绝对值得更多。是的。绝对。他在现实中真的是一个顶级的演员。

00:46:52.670 --> 00:46:57.890
<v John Hodgman>So you start the studio. You get a Bruce Campbell vehicle going and you hire me to do the book.
<v John Hodgman>所以你开了工作室。你进行一个Bruce Campbell的项目，然后你雇我来写书。

00:46:57.890 --> 00:46:59.470
<v Man 3>There you go. That's about it pretty much.
<v Man 3>这就对了。基本就是这样。

00:46:59.470 --> 00:47:00.350
<v John Hodgman>And then we'll all hang out.
<v John Hodgman>然后我们就一起混。

00:47:00.350 --> 00:47:02.650
<v Man 3>We got to hang out, definitely.
<v Man 3>我们一定要混在一起，绝对。

00:47:02.650 --> 00:47:28.210
<v John Hodgman>This, I, realize is the paradise that all Bruce Campbell fans want to reach. They all want to hang with Bruce, but they want to earn it. They want to help him. And if the book is a hit, then I will have done exactly what that kid dreams of. What I wanted to do all along, help, and then hang out.
<v John Hodgman>我意识到，这是所有Bruce Campbell粉丝想要到达的天堂。他们都想和Bruce混在一起，但他们想赢得它。他们想帮助他。如果书大卖，那么我将做到了那个孩子梦想的事情。我一直想做的事，帮忙，然后混在一起。

00:47:28.210 --> 00:47:49.940
<v John Hodgman>Finally, the convention is over. A few fans try to shadow Bruce up to his room, but he evades them. I, however, am still able to follow him through the back passages, the freight elevators, the restricted access doors. Then we run into some trouble. Bruce's key card won't open his room door and the organizer has to go down and have it reprogrammed at the desk.
<v John Hodgman>最后，大会结束了。一些粉丝试图跟踪Bruce到他的房间，但他避开了他们。然而，我仍然能够穿过后面的通道、货运电梯、限制进入的门跟随他。然后我们遇到了一些麻烦。Bruce的门卡打不开他的房门，组织者不得不下去在台前重新编程。

00:47:49.940 --> 00:48:03.960
<v John Hodgman>Bruce and I stand there in the shadowy hallway locked out of his room. He seems fidgety, exposed. There's still quite a few fans roaming around and now I realize I'm one of them. I'm not his agent anymore.
<v John Hodgman>Bruce和我站在阴影下的走廊里，被锁在房间外。他看起来有点坐立不安，暴露在外。仍然有相当多的粉丝在走来走去，现在我意识到我也是其中之一。我不再是他的代理人了。

00:48:03.960 --> 00:48:25.020
<v John Hodgman>When the organizer comes back with the key Bruce will go in, get changed, head out to the airport. I'll go back to my apartment, watch him on television just like I did before I ever met him. Pretty soon, I'll run out of plausible excuses to email him, to call him, to get the special treatment I've come to expect from my friend, the movie star.
<v John Hodgman>当组织者拿着钥匙回来时，Bruce会进去，换好衣服，前往机场。我会回到我的公寓，像在我见到他之前一样在电视上看着他。很快，我就没法找合理的借口给他发邮件、给他打电话，以获取我从我的朋友、电影明星那里所期望的特殊待遇了。

00:48:25.020 --> 00:48:30.180
<v John Hodgman>Is there anything you won't do for a fan?
<v John Hodgman>有什么事是你不会为粉丝做的吗？

00:48:30.180 --> 00:48:34.400
<v Bruce Campbell>They got in their head about saying these lines of dialogue.
<v Bruce Campbell>他们脑子里总是想着说这些台词。

00:48:34.400 --> 00:48:35.160
<v John Hodgman>Like what lines?
<v John Hodgman>什么台词？

00:48:35.160 --> 00:48:51.760
<v Bruce Campbell>Like groovy or give me some sugar or something. Those are tough just because it's hard to have the same feeling. And plus it's like, you've rented the movie. That's where it comes from. That's the original. I would just be giving you sloppy seconds.
<v Bruce Campbell>比如“groovy”或者“给我点糖果”之类的。那些很难说，因为很难有同样的感觉。而且就像，你租了那部电影。那是台词的出处。那是原创。我只是给你吃剩的。

00:48:51.760 --> 00:48:52.710
<v John Hodgman>Would you say groovy?
<v John Hodgman>你会说“groovy”吗？

00:48:52.710 --> 00:48:54.260
<v Bruce Campbell>No.
<v Bruce Campbell>不。

00:48:54.260 --> 00:48:54.820
<v John Hodgman>Come on.
<v John Hodgman>来吧。

00:48:54.820 --> 00:48:55.460
<v Bruce Campbell>No.
<v Bruce Campbell>不。

00:48:55.460 --> 00:48:56.780
<v John Hodgman>For your old literary agent.
<v John Hodgman>为你以前的文学代理人。

00:48:56.780 --> 00:49:04.750
<v Bruce Campbell>I said no.
<v Bruce Campbell>我说不。

00:49:04.750 --> 00:49:14.480
<v Ira Glass>John Hodgman lives in New York. Bruce Campbell's book is If Chins Could Kill: Confessions of a B-Movie Actor.
<v Ira Glass>John Hodgman住在纽约。Bruce Campbell的书是《如果下巴能杀人：B级片演员的自白》。

00:49:14.480 --> 00:49:51.460
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC-"THEME TO SECRET AGENT MAN"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐-“秘密特工主题曲”]

00:49:51.460 --> 00:49:57.410
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Airel Sharon, Shimon Peres, David Ben Gurion, and Me!
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，阿里尔·沙龙，希蒙·佩雷斯，大卫·本·古里安，还有我！

00:49:57.410 --> 00:50:06.030
<v Ira Glass>Well, you've heard about a number of different kinds of dreams in today's program. There is also a kind of dream that we have to protect ourselves from seeing who we really are.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，你在今天的节目中听到了许多不同种类的梦想。也有一种梦想，我们需要保护自己，以免看到我们真正是谁。

00:50:06.030 --> 00:50:47.010
<v Adam Davidson>December 3, 1986, Wednesday. Another fascinating day in the life of Adam Davidson. I have a math test tomorrow. I'm going to school early to tutor a girl in my class for the aforementioned test. My math class, a joint precalculus and calculus class, consists mainly of seniors not especially interested in learning. I guess that I'm the quote "class expert" unquote, in that I always do the math problems which no one else can. And for this, I'm disliked. I guess that because I apply myself, think clearly, and do a little work, as well as some intelligence helping out, I am a geek. In truth, I am far from it.
<v Adam Davidson>1986年12月3日，星期三。Adam Davidson生活中的又一个迷人日子。我明天有个数学考试。我早早去学校给班上的一个女孩补习刚才提到的考试。我的数学课，一门联合预微积分和微积分课程，主要由对学习不太感兴趣的三年级学生组成。我想我是所谓的“班级专家”，因为我总是能做别人做不到的数学题。因此，我不受欢迎。我想是因为我努力学习，思考清晰，做了一点工作，加上一些智力帮助，我成了极客。事实上，我远非如此。

00:50:47.010 --> 00:50:53.090
<v Ira Glass>When you first read that to yourself, when you first saw it, your reaction was?
<v Ira Glass>当你第一次对自己读到这个，当你第一次看到它时，你的反应是？

00:50:53.090 --> 00:50:55.490
<v Adam Davidson>It was pure horror.
<v Adam Davidson>纯粹的恐惧。

00:50:55.490 --> 00:51:12.950
<v Ira Glass>Recently, Adam Davidson, an occasional contributor to our program, found his old high school diaries. Adam's mom is Israeli, his dad is American. Adam grew up in New York. His body was in New York. His brain, as the diaries reveal, was somewhere else entirely.
<v Ira Glass>最近，Adam Davidson，我们节目的偶尔撰稿人，发现了他的高中日记。Adam的妈妈是以色列人，爸爸是美国人。Adam在纽约长大。他的身体在纽约。他的大脑，正如日记所揭示的，完全在另一个地方。

00:51:12.950 --> 00:51:44.810
<v Adam Davidson>I remember when I was writing it, I remember very clearly. Although I don't say this in the diary that it was very clear to me that this was the diary of the future prime minister of Israel. Me. That I would one day be prime minister. And it would be very important for history, for people to know the deep thoughts of a young Zionist as he prepared his way to lead his nation.
<v Adam Davidson>我记得我写它的时候，记得非常清楚。尽管我在日记里没这么说，但很清楚的是，这是一位以色列未来总理的日记。我。我有一天会成为总理。对于历史来说，对于人们了解一位年轻的犹太复国主义者在准备领导他的国家时的深层思想，这将非常重要。

00:51:44.810 --> 00:51:53.260
<v Ira Glass>Now, our regular listeners here in This American Life might remember that you've been on our program describing your experience in Israeli army summer camp.
<v Ira Glass>现在，我们《美国生活》的常客可能记得你曾经在我们的节目中描述过你在以色列军队夏令营的经历。

00:51:53.260 --> 00:51:56.970
<v Adam Davidson>That was right before I started writing this diary.
<v Adam Davidson>那是在我开始写这本日记之前。

00:51:56.970 --> 00:51:58.030
<v Ira Glass>Read me another.
<v Ira Glass>再给我读一段。

00:51:58.030 --> 00:52:02.990
<v Adam Davidson>Sure. Let's see.
<v Adam Davidson>好的。看看。

00:52:02.990 --> 00:52:26.350
<v Adam Davidson>There's so much wrong with Jews in Israel that I'm going to have a job ahead of me. One thing is the lack of any strong Jewish identity among most Jews. This attitude sickens me. You Jews of the world, stop worrying about money and well being. I do not know what exactly I'll do. But if this situation continues when I'm a bit older, then watch out world Jewry, here comes Adam.
<v Adam Davidson>以色列的犹太人有很多问题，我将面临一项艰巨的工作。其中之一是大多数犹太人缺乏任何强大的犹太认同感。这种态度让我感到恶心。你们世界各地的犹太人，别再担心钱和福利了。我不知道我具体要做什么。但如果这种情况持续到我再大一点的时候，那么世界犹太人要注意了，Adam来了。

00:52:26.350 --> 00:52:32.890
<v Adam Davidson>And "watch out world Jewry, here comes Adam" was all in capital letters.
<v Adam Davidson>而且“世界犹太人要注意了，Adam来了”全部是大写字母。

00:52:32.890 --> 00:52:46.770
<v Ira Glass>Wow. It's interesting that you actually are addressing a readership.
<v Ira Glass>哇。有趣的是你实际上是在面对读者。

00:52:46.770 --> 00:52:50.170
<v Adam Davidson>I know. That's what kind of amazing.
<v Adam Davidson>我知道。这太令人惊奇了。

00:52:50.170 --> 00:52:54.860
<v Ira Glass>And that readership is world Jewry.
<v Ira Glass>而且那些读者是世界犹太人。

00:52:54.860 --> 00:53:03.950
<v Adam Davidson>Yeah. The Jews of the world will one day read this book and will say, if he knew this at 16, how could I be living so badly?
<v Adam Davidson>是的。世界各地的犹太人有一天会读到这本书，并会说，如果他在16岁时就知道这一点，我怎么能活得这么糟糕？

00:53:03.950 --> 00:53:08.950
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask you to just read one of the passages where you talk about Israel?
<v Ira Glass>我可以请你读一下你谈论以色列的段落吗？

00:53:08.950 --> 00:53:15.900
<v Adam Davidson>Sure. Let's see. I mean I have this thing from January 4, 1987.
<v Adam Davidson>当然。看看。我的意思是，我有1987年1月4日的这段东西。

00:53:15.900 --> 00:53:34.500
<v Adam Davidson>I memorized "The Hope", Hatikva, which is the Israeli National Anthem a few minutes ago. That will help me in Israel. I find that really amazing that here I am, the future prime minister of Israel and what are the things I need? Oh, God, I need to know the national anthem. I'll probably be called upon to recite that at some point.
<v Adam Davidson>我刚才背诵了《希望》，Hatikva，也就是以色列国歌。那会在以色列帮到我。我觉得那真的很神奇，我在这里，以色列的未来总理，我需要什么东西？噢，上帝，我需要知道国歌。我可能在某个时候需要背诵那个。

00:53:34.500 --> 00:53:37.420
<v Ira Glass>Or they'll be a ball game or something, you'll need to stand up and sing it.
<v Ira Glass>或者会有球赛之类的，你需要站起来唱它。

00:53:37.420 --> 00:53:42.460
<v Adam Davidson>Right, exactly.
<v Adam Davidson>对，完全正确。

00:53:42.460 --> 00:54:15.270
<v Adam Davidson>At 16, I had such an inflated sense of myself. There was so much going on in my life then that I can remember and I wasn't recording it. Instead, I was creating this ridiculous fantasy of I'm not just a 16 year old kid who's having crushes and a hopeless geek who can't get a girl to kiss him. And being scared and confused about growing old. I'm the future prime minister of Israel and everything goes through that.
<v Adam Davidson>16岁时，我对自己有一种极度膨胀的感觉。那时我的生活中发生了很多事，我可以记得，但我没有记录。相反，我创造了这个荒谬的幻想：我不只是一个16岁的小孩，有着迷恋对象，一个无法让女孩吻他的无可救药的极客。害怕和困惑于变老。我是以色列的未来总理，一切都通过那个滤镜。

00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:16.970
<v Adam Davidson>But I don't know.
<v Adam Davidson>但我不知道。

00:54:16.970 --> 00:54:32.670
<v Ira Glass>But maybe keeping a diary where one tells the truth, maybe that's a luxury of being a certain kind of person in a certain kind of situation. Maybe other people in another kind of situation need to actually make up a little fantastic.
<v Ira Glass>但也许坚持写一本讲真话的日记，那可能是特定情况下特定人的奢侈。也许处于另一种情况下的其他人需要真正地编造一点幻想。

00:54:32.670 --> 00:55:07.830
<v Announcer>Yeah. I didn't have much angst about being the future prime minister of Israel. I was very calm and confident and comfortable with it. And I had so much angst about every other aspect of my life. So I now see it as just kind of-- maybe it was a good solution. It was a good way to deal with what I was going through. To have this space where I could just be one of the greats.
<v Announcer>是的。对于成为以色列的未来总理，我没什么焦虑。我非常冷静、自信并对此感到舒适。而我对自己生活中的所有其他方面都有很多焦虑。所以我现在把它视为一种——也许这是一个好办法。这是处理我所经历的事情的一个好方法。拥有这个空间，我可以成为伟人之一。

00:55:07.830 --> 00:55:19.660
<v Ira Glass>I wonder what the 16 year old Adam Davidson would feel in knowing that finally, an audience of a million people was getting some of the reading from this diary.
<v Ira Glass>我想知道16岁的Adam Davidson在知道终于有100万听众读到这本日记的内容时会是什么感觉。

00:55:19.660 --> 00:55:28.620
<v Adam Davidson>I think this would feel so small to that 16 year old. This would feel so nothing. This would be so unimportant.
<v Adam Davidson>我觉得这对那个16岁的人来说太小了。这感觉简直什么都不是。这会很不重要。

00:55:28.620 --> 00:55:30.070
<v Ira Glass>Being on the radio?
<v Ira Glass>在广播上？

00:55:30.070 --> 00:55:44.400
<v Adam Davidson>Being on the radio, a million people. What's a million people? I mean, we're talking about history. We're talking about sweeping changes. I'm pretty sure that he would be thoroughly unimpressed.
<v Adam Davidson>在广播上，100万人。100万人是什么？我的意思是，我们在谈论历史。我们在谈论彻底的变革。我很确定他会完全不为所动。

00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:49.300
<v Ira Glass>And Adam, what would the 16 year old you think of you now?
<v Ira Glass>那么Adam，16岁的你会怎么看现在的你？

00:55:49.300 --> 00:55:54.260
<v Adam Davidson>I think he'd be really disappointed. I think he'd be really sad.
<v Adam Davidson>我觉得他会很失望。我觉得他会很伤心。

00:55:54.260 --> 00:55:55.850
<v Ira Glass>Because you're not the prime minister of Israel?
<v Ira Glass>因为你不是以色列总理？

00:55:55.850 --> 00:56:01.820
<v Adam Davidson>Yeah, because I just have such a small life.
<v Adam Davidson>是的，因为我过着这么渺小的生活。

00:56:01.820 --> 00:56:44.020
<v Adam Davidson>I mean I remember I was very disappointed and very sad about my parents. I was reading biographies, of course, of all the prime ministers of Israel. And I would just think about my parents and just think, how do you wake up every day knowing that your actions won't affect millions of people? How is that enough motivation? Just to have your petty little craft and your petty little family and your small little apartment. It just seemed pathetic. I mean, they have the kind of life that, I mean basically, I want for myself.
<v Adam Davidson>我的意思是，我记得我对自己父母感到非常失望和悲伤。我当时当然在读以色列所有总理的传记。我会想到我的父母，只是想，你每天醒来怎么能知道你的行为不会影响数百万人？怎么能有足够的动力？仅仅为了你那微不足道的技艺，你那微不足道的家庭和你那渺小的公寓。这看起来真可悲。我的意思是，他们过着那种，我基本上想要的生活。

00:56:44.020 --> 00:56:52.140
<v Ira Glass>What you're saying though, is that the 16 year old you would be cringing at your 30 year old version just as your 30 year old version is cringing at the 16.
<v Ira Glass>不过，你说的是，16岁的你会对30岁的版本感到畏缩，正如你30岁的版本对16岁的感到畏缩一样。

00:56:52.140 --> 00:57:08.310
<v Adam Davidson>Yeah, that's very true. He would be very, very disgusted if he heard this radio piece. It would seem like I had settled in a pathetic way.
<v Adam Davidson>是的，那非常真实。如果他听到这期广播节目，他会非常、非常厌恶。这看起来就像我以一种可悲的方式妥协了。

00:57:08.310 --> 00:57:12.670
<v Ira Glass>Adam Davidson in Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>Adam Davidson在洛杉矶。

00:57:12.670 --> 00:57:33.460
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Jonathan Goldstein and myself, with Alex Blumberg, Wendy Dorr and Starlee Kine. Senior producer Julie Snyder. Elizabeth Meister runs our website. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Paul McCarthy. Original music in our story on FoxxJazell by TRS-80. That's Deb, Kent, and Jay.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们的节目今天由Jonathan Goldstein和我本人制作，Alex Blumberg，Wendy Dorr和Starlee Kine参与。高级制作人Julie Snyder。Elizabeth Meister运行我们的网站。Todd Bachmann和Paul McCarthy提供制作帮助。FoxxJazell故事中的原创音乐由TRS-80提供。那是Deb，Kent和Jay。

00:57:33.460 --> 00:57:48.290
<v Ira Glass>To buy a cassette of this program, call us here at WBEZ in Chicago 312-832-3380. Or visit our website where you can also listen to our programs for free at www.thisamericanlife.org. Don't forget those MP3s.
<v Ira Glass>要购买本节目的磁带，请拨打我们在芝加哥WBEZ的电话312-832-3380。或者访问我们的网站，您也可以在www.thisamericanlife.org免费收听我们的节目。别忘了那些MP3。

00:57:48.290 --> 00:57:51.510
